limiting by external dependency B, which directly rejected roughly one-third (31%) of incoming outbound calls with HTTP 429 status codes (E05). This failure mode was fundamentally external or downstream interface saturation rather than an internal processing defect, host crash, or software deployment error (E10). However, the incident severity was heavily compounded by two major amplifying factors: uncoordinated retry storms and latency elongation. When dependency B began throttling, tasks were held in memory while attempting repeated calls using exponential backoff up to three attempts (E06). This retry loop generated a 420% explosion in total call volume, creating a self-reinforcing congestion collapse. The retry storm transformed a partial dependency degradation into an internal bottleneck where workers spent the vast majority of their runtime blocked on sleeping or retrying downstream requests, driving median task latency from 1.8 seconds to 14.2 seconds (E03). The queue growth between 09:00 and 09:10 (E02) reflects initial queue buildup that coincided with or slightly preceded explicit 429 telemetry recording at 09:15, indicating that latent downstream latency or early unrecorded rate contention preceded full error thresholds.\n\nAt 09:20, operators attempted horizontal worker scaling, increasing worker concurrency from 8 to 16 (E07). Under normal compute-bound or internal lock-bound constraints, doubling compute capacity would dramatically improve throughput. In this scenario, doubling workers yielded an insignificant 6% reduction in queue depth over a ten-minute observation window. This failure to remediate occurred because the processing bottleneck was entirely downstream and quota-governed. Host utilization metrics at 09:14 (CPU 43%, memory 51%, disk 22%) already proved that worker execution was not starved for host-level resources (E04). By increasing the active worker count from 8 to 16, the system doubled the concurrency of outbound requests directed at 